      "dom_id": "question-7611",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is the center of the ellipse  $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=3$ and $k=2,$ so the center is located at $(3,2).$",
